<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Stupinigi Hunting Lodge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi is a royal hunting lodge south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a> for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> and begun in 1729. Its star-shaped plan centres on an oval hall under a dome crowned by a bronze stag. Used by the house of Savoy for hunts, festivities and weddings, it is part of the Residences of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palazzina-di-caccia-of-stupinigi.jpg" alt="Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paris Orlando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior_of_the_Palazzina_di_caccia_of_Stupinigi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Stupinigi is now a frazione of the town of Nichelino, 10 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. The original castle there belonged until 1418 to the Acaja line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, Lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, was sold to Marquis Rolando Pallavicino in 1493, and was acquired by Emmanuel Philibert in 1563, when he moved the ducal capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> to Turin. He also took from the Pallavicini family almost 1,700 hectares of land and woods around it as a hunting estate. Work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a>'s new lodge went far enough in two years for the first formal hunt to be held in 1731.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Juvarra's plan follows the arms of a St Andrew's Cross: four angled wings, holding the royal and guest apartments, spread from a double-height oval hall, aligned with a tree-lined avenue from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. The hall was finished in 1730, and from 1731 to 1733 the Bolognese brothers Giuseppe and Domenico Valeriani painted its vault with the Triumph of Diana, goddess of the hunt. Francesco Ladatte made the bronze stag on the dome in 1766; the original was moved into the Portrait Gallery in 1992 and a copy now stands on the roof.</p><p>Under Charles Emmanuel III and Victor Amadeus III the lodge and its park kept growing, first under Juvarra's assistant Giovanni Tommaso Prunotto and then under architects such as Benedetto Alfieri, until it had 137 rooms and 17 galleries. The French gardener Michael Benard laid out the walled park in 1740. The interiors are Italian Rococo, with lacquer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, gilded stucco and mirrors. The French engraver Charles-Nicolas Cochin criticised their abundance of decoration, and Jérôme Lalande called the hall the dream of an architect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> preferred Stupinigi for celebrations and dynastic weddings: there, in 1773, Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of Savoy married Charles Philippe, Count of Artois, the future Charles X of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Pauline Bonaparte, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s sister, fitted out a cabinet with a marble bath while her husband Camillo Borghese was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. The building now houses the Museo di Arte e Ammobiliamento, with furniture by Pietro Piffetti and Giuseppe Maria Bonzanigo, and since 1992 the surrounding woods and farmland have been protected as the Parco naturale di Stupinigi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palazzina di caccia of Stupinigi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with arcaded streets and squares; Guarino Guarini and later Filippo Juvarra, who designed the Basilica of Superga, the hunting lodge of <b>Stupinigi</b> and the façade of Palazzo Madama, made it one of the architectural capitals of Europe, and the Savoy residences are now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
